{"name":"Mineral Red","hex":"#b35457","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"mineral","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any naturally occurring solid material that has a (more or less) definite chemical composition and characteristic physical properties; especially, an inorganic and crystalline one.","Any inorganic material (as distinguished from animal or vegetable)."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["of, related to, or containing minerals"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mineral"},{"term":"red","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of the setting sun, blood, and strawberries; the color which is evoked by the longest visible wavelengths (between about 625–740 nm), and a primary additive color.","A revolutionary socialist or (most commonly) a Communist;  a Bolshevik, a supporter of the Bolsheviks in the Russian Civil War."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a red hue.","Having an orange-brown or orange-blond colour; ginger."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/red"}],"entries":[{"title":"Mineral","blocks":[{"text":"In geology and mineralogy, a mineral or mineral species is, broadly speaking, a solid substance with a fairly well-defined chemical composition and a specific crystal structure that occurs naturally in pure form."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/S%C3%A9randite%2C_natrolite%2C_analcime%2C_aegirine_300-4-2112.JPG/330px-S%C3%A9randite%2C_natrolite%2C_analcime%2C_aegirine_300-4-2112.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mineral","wikidata":"Q7946"},{"title":"Red","blocks":[{"text":"Red is the color at the long wavelength end of the visible spectrum of light, next to orange and opposite violet. It has a dominant wavelength of approximately 625–750 nanometers. It is a primary color in the RGB color model and a secondary color in the CMYK color model, and is the complementary color of cyan. Reds range from the brilliant yellow-tinged scarlet and vermillion to bluish-red crimson, and vary in shade from the pale red pink to the dark red burgundy."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Delicious_fresh_strawberries_%284735910403%29.jpg/330px-Delicious_fresh_strawberries_%284735910403%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red","wikidata":"Q3142"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Sillimanite-199671.jpg/500px-Sillimanite-199671.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Sillimanite-199671.jpg/500px-Sillimanite-199671.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Schist is a metamorphic rock characterized by an abundance of platy minerals. In this example, the rock has prominent sillimanite porphyroblasts as large as 3 cm (1.2 in).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASillimanite-199671.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/Hubnerite-Quartz-216455.jpg/330px-Hubnerite-Quartz-216455.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/Hubnerite-Quartz-216455.jpg/330px-Hubnerite-Quartz-216455.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Hübnerite, the manganese-rich end-member of the wolframite series, with minor quartz in the background","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHubnerite-Quartz-216455.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Kaolinite-Orthoclase-lw18c.jpg/330px-Kaolinite-Orthoclase-lw18c.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Kaolinite-